From: Kuzma30 <kuzemkoa@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:09:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/24] Modeling Algorithms - Close a fillet with a cap on the
 face at its end

At a vertex with more than three edges, the end of a single fillet was
built by PerformIntersectionAtEnd, which intersects the fillet with every
face around the vertex. When the faces between the two support faces do
not reach the fillet (the corner of a pocket whose walls have chamfered
edges, FreeCAD issue 31894), it fell back to a plate patch, which only
approximates the corner.

There the fillet is cut across by the face beyond the arc where one of its
sides leaves the support. PerformCapOnEndFace detects this case before
anything is stored: the face at end does not share an edge with the
opposite support face at the vertex, the fillet on the opposite face
reaches it (at the end of its trace at this vertex and at its middle; the
other end may stop at another such face, as for a torus capped at both
ends), and no edge at the vertex outside the two support faces meets the
fillet. The corner is then built by PerformOneCorner as in the OnSame
state: the face at end is extended up to the fillet, and the opposite face
is limited by its intersection with the face at end, started at the vertex
itself since there is no edge to prolong. The orientation of that curve,
which came from the prolonged edge, is computed from the sides of the
faces to keep, at the point of the curve nearest to an inside point.

The face at end may be any surface; the side of the surface is found by
projection. When the direction of the cap curve at the vertex follows an
edge of the opposite face, the cap would only double that edge (the end
face of FreeCAD issue 27807 meets the opposite face on its boundary), so
such a vertex keeps the old way. The normal of a face at the vertex is
taken only for a face that reaches the vertex.

Tests bugs/modalg_8/fillet_cap_on_cylinder_at_pocket_corner and
fillet_torus_capped_at_pocket_walls; GTests for the planar case.

